5. TWO-WAY AUDIO STREAMING

The interaction must support:

User voice → AI processing → AI audio response

The assistant must be able to communicate using audio only.

Text transcription and response text can be displayed as optional visual assistance, but audio must be the primary interaction method.

Input audio

Implement:

- Microphone permission handling.
- Audio recording initialization.
- PCM/audio format configuration based on the selected Gemini API.
- Appropriate sample rate and channel configuration.
- Audio chunk generation.
- Streaming audio transmission.
- Audio buffering.
- Backpressure handling.
- Audio interruption.
- Recording cleanup.

Do not record the entire conversation into a large byte array.

Stream audio in bounded chunks.

Do not block the main thread while recording, processing, or transmitting audio.

Audio chunk management

Implement a proper streaming pipeline.

Requirements:

- Fixed or appropriately bounded chunk sizes.
- Correct audio format.
- Sequential chunk ordering.
- Appropriate timestamps or sequence information where required.
- Bounded buffers.
- Backpressure handling.
- Safe cancellation.
- No unbounded queues.
- No unnecessary audio copies.
- Proper handling of partial chunks.
- Correct end-of-turn/end-of-input signaling according to the API.

Use a producer-consumer approach where appropriate.

Avoid creating a new coroutine for every audio chunk.

Noise reduction and audio quality

Investigate Android-supported audio processing capabilities.

Use appropriate platform-supported audio effects when available, such as:

- Noise suppression.
- Acoustic echo cancellation.
- Automatic gain control.

Check availability before enabling effects.

Do not assume these effects exist on every device.

Ensure audio effects are attached to the correct audio session and released properly.

Do not apply multiple overlapping audio-processing mechanisms without understanding their impact.

Document device-specific limitations.

